ABOUT: Welcome to the commentary track!


prop:C-MOVE: This property does double duty. For rooms, it specifies extra travel time for particular directions. (That is, unusually long exit paths.)
	For the dispenser buttons on the [~obj:CHEMICAL-DISPENSER], `C-MOVE` specifies the chemical color.
	(In fact *Planetfall* only uses 26 of the 31 properties, so there’s no need to double up like this.)


glob:LOAD-ALLOWED: The maximum weight you can carry. This decreases as you become sicker; see [rtn:I-SICKNESS-WARNINGS].
	Most Infocom games track your initial weight limit (as `LOAD-MAX`). This allows the game to restore `LOAD-ALLOWED` when you heal. But in *Planetfall*, you never return to full health! So it is sufficient to track incremental changes.

glob:C-TABLE: The timer table. See the “Timers” tab for a current listing.

TIMERS-LEGEND: A list of the timers running (or paused) in the timer table ([~glob:C-TABLE]). Positive numbers count down (while enabled) and call the named function after that many millichrons. Negative numbers are called every turn while the timer is enabled.
	(*Planetfall* is the first Infocom game in which timers can count down by arbitrary amounts of time. *Deadline* fakes its long `WAIT`s by calling [~rtn:CLOCKER] once per minute.)
	This list only grows as you play; timers can be added, started, and stopped, but not removed.

rtn:I-RANDOM-INTERRUPTS: This timer sets up various random factors (the [explosion timer|rtn:I-BLOWUP-FEINSTEIN], the [combination lock|glob:NUMBER-NEEDED], etc) – but only *after* the player’s first move.
	Why? Almost certainly to get better random numbers. On an eight-bit home computer, the only decent source of randomness is human input. (See the [Apple II manual|https://archive.org/details/apple-ii-ref-manual/page/33/mode/1up?q=rdkey] for an example.) On such machines, the `RANDOM` opcode relies on keyboard timing to ensure that random numbers really are unpredictable.
	(In this exhibit, `RANDOM` uses Javascript’s `Math.random()` function, so the issue doesn’t arise.)

rtn:I-BLOWUP-FEINSTEIN: This is queued 240-330 millichrons after the first turn. Once it fires, it re-queues itself to run every turn for the next five turns, narrating the ship's destruction.

rtn:I-POD-TRIP: This timer handles the pod’s trip to Resida. (Always twelve turns, regardless of your actions.)

rtn:I-SINK-POD: This timer handles the pod falling into the water and sinking. It’s triggered when you leave the [~obj:SAFETY-WEB].

rtn:I-SICKNESS-WARNINGS: The timer that handles the progression of The Disease.
	This fires every 700 millichrons, but it only has effect if [glob:SICKNESS-WARNING-FLAG] is set. That flag is set [after sleeping|~rtn:WAKING-UP] and after your [first shuttle ride|~rtn:LAWANDA-PLATFORM-F]. The effect is that you feel worse once a day (or after that shuttle ride), but with an unpredictable delay.
	
glob:SICKNESS-WARNING-FLAG: Indicates whether your [~glob:SICKNESS-LEVEL] is about to get worse. This flag is set [after sleeping|~rtn:WAKING-UP] and after your [first shuttle ride|~rtn:LAWANDA-PLATFORM-F]. It takes effect the next time [rtn:I-SICKNESS-WARNINGS] fires. (So up to 700 millichrons after sleeping, etc.)


PHONETIC: The printed language uses a phonetic spelling system. Long vowels are always doubled; “X” means “th”; “C” means “ch”; and so on. There are no silent letters.
	Amusingly, the Residan *spoken* language remains identical to Galalingua. (Floyd doesn’t seem to have an accent. Nor do [recorded announcements|~rtn:I-ANNOUNCEMENT].) This is somewhat unrealistic; real languages tend to change pronunciation faster than spelling, which is why the tough coughs as he ploughs dough.

rtn:PLAQUE-PSEUDO: See [notes on foonetik speleeng|PHONETIC].
	The Translation tab lets you see this text in regular English. I mean Galalingua.

glob:MAIN-MENU: When *Planetfall* was first released in 1983, it was billed as “INTERLOGIC Science Fiction”, in keeping with Infocom’s other games. The “Interlogic” trademark was deleted in this 1985 release. But the in-game library text was never updated to match.

glob:34-TEXT: A reference to *Suspended* as in-game history. (As distinct from the reference in “[Starkros and Suspendid|glob:63-TEXT]”, which is not exactly in-character.)
	Note that Floyd is a [B-19|~src:COMPONE-2153].

glob:51-TEXT: The “lab leak” story is a lot more fraught than it came off as in the 1980s.

glob:62-TEXT: The entry refers to “Witness” rather than “*The* Witness”. I know I was annoyed when Infocom broke its pattern of one-word titles. Perhaps Meretzky agreed.

glob:63-TEXT: But see also “[Roobotiks|glob:34-TEXT]”.


glob:DIAL-NUMBER: The current setting of the [~obj:COMBINATION-DIAL].

glob:NUMBER-NEEDED: The correct combination for the [~obj:COMBINATION-DIAL]. The value is set *after* your first move; see [rtn:I-RANDOM-INTERRUPTS].
	Note that peeking at this value in the State tab won’t help you much! The [~obj:TELEPORTATION-CARD] is in the same place as the [~obj:COMBINATION-PAPER] clue. This must have been a guard against brute-force guessing; it serves equally well against Visible Zorker cheats.


